1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,190
You try to delete a file.

2
00:00:02,780 --> 00:00:05,410
Your computer says it is
open in another program.

3
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:07,630
It does not say which one.

4
00:00:08,410 --> 00:00:11,780
That message is the visible end
of a much stranger question.

5
00:00:12,390 --> 00:00:15,120
What does it actually mean to open a file?

6
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:18,960
Because on another computer,
the same file, open

7
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:21,390
in the same way, deletes
without complaining.

8
00:00:22,660 --> 00:00:24,450
Same file. Same action.

9
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:26,710
Two completely different answers.

10
00:00:29,243 --> 00:00:32,473
Start with the part that annoys
everybody. Which program?

11
00:00:33,457 --> 00:00:34,657
Windows knows.

12
00:00:35,317 --> 00:00:39,647
It has a kernel object for that open
file, and it knows which process owns it.

13
00:00:40,197 --> 00:00:41,467
It could name it.

14
00:00:42,407 --> 00:00:44,097
So you close things at random.

15
00:00:44,887 --> 00:00:46,397
You open Task Manager.

16
00:00:47,187 --> 00:00:50,877
You stare at a list of processes
and recognise hardly any of them.

17
00:00:51,877 --> 00:00:55,327
And for years, the answer really
was to download a separate tool.

18
00:00:56,137 --> 00:01:01,167
Unlocker came out in 2005, and on
Windows XP that was the answer.

19
00:01:02,397 --> 00:01:06,987
Then Windows 7 shipped
Resource Monitor. The CPU tab.

20
00:01:07,617 --> 00:01:09,767
A list called Associated Handles.

21
00:01:10,487 --> 00:01:12,037
Type the name of the file.

22
00:01:12,787 --> 00:01:16,877
It gives you the program and the
process ID. Nothing to install.

23
00:01:17,707 --> 00:01:19,557
It has been there since 2009.

24
00:01:22,777 --> 00:01:25,517
Although the separate tools
did more than just tell you.

25
00:01:25,597 --> 00:01:28,727
They could close the
handle and let the file go.

26
00:01:30,367 --> 00:01:32,257
Which brings us back to the question.

27
00:01:33,187 --> 00:01:35,877
What did that program acquire
when it opened the file?

28
00:01:36,857 --> 00:01:40,387
Say a video player opens
a file called movie.mp4.

29
00:01:41,137 --> 00:01:44,747
On Windows, opening a file
means filling in two things.

30
00:01:45,917 --> 00:01:51,467
The first one is obvious. What do I want
to do with it? Read it. Write to it.

31
00:01:52,177 --> 00:01:54,267
The second one is the interesting one.

32
00:01:54,897 --> 00:01:57,407
What may everybody else
do while I have it open?

33
00:01:58,467 --> 00:01:59,997
That is a real setting.

34
00:02:00,807 --> 00:02:03,737
You say what other programs
may do while you have it open.

35
00:02:04,807 --> 00:02:07,417
They may read it. They
may also write to it.

36
00:02:07,767 --> 00:02:09,697
They may delete or rename it.

37
00:02:10,567 --> 00:02:14,857
Or nothing at all. Nobody
touches this while I am using it.

38
00:02:16,207 --> 00:02:19,387
The flag that permits deleting
is called FILE_SHARE_DELETE.

39
00:02:19,967 --> 00:02:23,877
And Microsoft's documentation
adds one line underneath it.

40
00:02:24,757 --> 00:02:28,287
Delete access allows both
delete and rename operations.

41
00:02:29,567 --> 00:02:33,717
So a program that refuses delete
sharing has blocked renaming as well.

42
00:02:35,997 --> 00:02:37,477
And if the program that opened the file

43
00:02:37,557 --> 00:02:40,467
did not grant it,
nothing else can take it.

44
00:02:41,547 --> 00:02:44,117
Which is why the error
is not called file busy.

45
00:02:44,987 --> 00:02:46,777
It is called a sharing violation.

46
00:02:48,057 --> 00:02:51,087
The answer was sitting in the
name of the error the whole time.

47
00:02:53,967 --> 00:02:56,137
Now the same thing on a Linux machine.

48
00:02:57,007 --> 00:02:58,597
A player opens the video.

49
00:02:59,377 --> 00:03:02,287
And you type rm movie.mp4.

50
00:03:02,737 --> 00:03:04,747
It works. The video keeps playing.

51
00:03:07,277 --> 00:03:10,347
So what did that program get,
if not a hold on the file?

52
00:03:11,417 --> 00:03:13,487
This is the whole idea of the video.

53
00:03:14,197 --> 00:03:15,707
The name is not the file.

54
00:03:17,807 --> 00:03:19,417
movie.mp4 is a line in a directory.

55
00:03:20,487 --> 00:03:23,617
That line points at an object
which holds the actual data.

56
00:03:24,807 --> 00:03:28,617
When a program opens the file, what
it gets is a reference to the object.

57
00:03:28,887 --> 00:03:30,657
Not to the line.

58
00:03:31,597 --> 00:03:35,747
So removing the file deletes the
line. The object is untouched.

59
00:03:36,887 --> 00:03:39,337
The manual page says it in one sentence.

60
00:03:40,287 --> 00:03:42,207
The file will remain
in existence until the

61
00:03:42,287 --> 00:03:45,017
last file descriptor
referring to it is closed.

62
00:03:48,007 --> 00:03:50,617
Which sounds abstract
until you watch it happen.

63
00:03:51,387 --> 00:03:53,267
So I made a log file
five hundred megabytes

64
00:03:53,347 --> 00:03:56,557
long, and left one
process holding it open.

65
00:03:57,757 --> 00:03:59,207
Then I deleted it.

66
00:04:00,017 --> 00:04:02,387
Listing the directory
shows nothing at all.

67
00:04:03,037 --> 00:04:06,147
The tool that measures the
size of the folder says zero.

68
00:04:07,227 --> 00:04:09,287
And the tool that measures
free space on the disk

69
00:04:09,367 --> 00:04:11,837
says five hundred megabytes
are still missing.

70
00:04:13,217 --> 00:04:16,327
An empty folder. Half a gigabyte gone.

71
00:04:19,197 --> 00:04:23,627
This happens often enough that there is a
command just for finding files like this.

72
00:04:24,237 --> 00:04:26,807
Deleted, invisible, still on the disk.

73
00:04:27,937 --> 00:04:31,867
And the space comes back the moment
that one process lets go of the file.

74
00:04:34,607 --> 00:04:36,617
The same logic covers renaming.

75
00:04:37,147 --> 00:04:40,677
Move the file while it is open,
and the program never notices.

76
00:04:41,927 --> 00:04:44,037
The manual is blunt about that too.

77
00:04:45,127 --> 00:04:47,797
Open file descriptors for
the old path are unaffected.

78
00:04:49,037 --> 00:04:50,587
It was never using the name.

79
00:04:52,767 --> 00:04:54,997
Which has one limit worth knowing about.

80
00:04:55,487 --> 00:04:58,177
You cannot overwrite a
running program in place.

81
00:04:59,437 --> 00:05:01,847
Linux refuses. Text file busy.

82
00:05:04,133 --> 00:05:07,503
What you can do is detach the
name and put a new file there.

83
00:05:08,543 --> 00:05:12,553
The old process keeps running from
an object that no longer has a name.

84
00:05:13,243 --> 00:05:15,773
Anything started later gets the new one.

85
00:05:16,733 --> 00:05:19,443
Which is the same idea
again, proving itself.

86
00:05:20,353 --> 00:05:23,543
You can replace many things without
touching the object in place.

87
00:05:26,493 --> 00:05:29,783
Which is why Linux can update
a program while it is running.

88
00:05:30,513 --> 00:05:33,583
You just do not get the new
version until you restart it.

89
00:05:35,873 --> 00:05:38,043
So is Windows simply the worse design?

90
00:05:39,203 --> 00:05:42,073
No. And this is the part
that hardly anyone knows.

91
00:05:43,623 --> 00:05:46,393
Windows has a way to do
exactly the same thing.

92
00:05:47,383 --> 00:05:51,013
On NTFS, Windows can ask
for a POSIX style delete.

93
00:05:52,243 --> 00:05:55,153
Microsoft describes what
happens when you use it.

94
00:05:55,803 --> 00:05:58,073
The name is removed from
the visible namespace.

95
00:05:59,483 --> 00:06:02,303
And applications that already
had the file open can still

96
00:06:02,383 --> 00:06:05,973
use it, even though the name
they used to open it is gone.

97
00:06:07,183 --> 00:06:08,853
That is the Unix behaviour.

98
00:06:09,083 --> 00:06:10,183
On NTFS.

99
00:06:10,263 --> 00:06:11,933
Documented by Microsoft.

100
00:06:14,754 --> 00:06:16,804
Both systems can do both things.

101
00:06:17,614 --> 00:06:19,064
What differs is the default.

102
00:06:19,874 --> 00:06:22,884
What you get when you open a
file and never think about it.

103
00:06:23,804 --> 00:06:25,534
On Windows, you get a contract.

104
00:06:26,044 --> 00:06:28,634
On Unix, you get a reference to an object.

105
00:06:31,644 --> 00:06:33,774
Which sounds like a
question for programmers,

106
00:06:34,024 --> 00:06:36,274
until you have seen
this particular message.

107
00:06:37,434 --> 00:06:40,664
Please restart your computer
to finish installing updates.

108
00:06:43,244 --> 00:06:46,104
Suppose Windows has to
replace a library that a

109
00:06:46,184 --> 00:06:49,094
running process holds open
under the strict contract.

110
00:06:50,364 --> 00:06:52,954
It cannot swap the file
underneath that process.

111
00:06:53,544 --> 00:06:55,494
So something has to let go.

112
00:06:56,504 --> 00:06:59,144
Quit the application. Stop the service.

113
00:06:59,224 --> 00:07:01,994
Or hand the whole job
to the Restart Manager.

114
00:07:03,094 --> 00:07:04,604
Which is a real component.

115
00:07:05,334 --> 00:07:07,064
It shuts things down in order.

116
00:07:07,774 --> 00:07:10,404
Windows applications,
then console programs,

117
00:07:11,365 --> 00:07:13,955
then services, and Windows Explorer last.

118
00:07:14,105 --> 00:07:16,335
Then it starts them up again.

119
00:07:17,125 --> 00:07:19,285
And it exists for one
reason, which Microsoft

120
00:07:19,365 --> 00:07:21,815
states plainly in its own documentation.

121
00:07:23,385 --> 00:07:25,245
The primary reason that
software installation

122
00:07:25,325 --> 00:07:27,915
and updates require a system restart...

123
00:07:29,555 --> 00:07:31,735
is that some of the
files being updated are

124
00:07:31,815 --> 00:07:35,025
currently in use by a running
application or service.

125
00:07:38,295 --> 00:07:40,025
It would be too neat to stop there.

126
00:07:40,635 --> 00:07:44,405
Sharing rules are not the only
reason a computer asks to restart.

127
00:07:45,505 --> 00:07:47,295
A kernel update is different.

128
00:07:47,945 --> 00:07:50,335
You cannot swap the
kernel underneath itself.

129
00:07:51,415 --> 00:07:56,165
Linux has that problem too, although live
patching can sometimes avoid a reboot.

130
00:07:57,355 --> 00:08:00,565
So the honest version claims
less than the satisfying one.

131
00:08:03,265 --> 00:08:05,925
The way Windows treats files
in use made some components

132
00:08:06,005 --> 00:08:09,255
harder to replace while
running, and that contributed.

133
00:08:10,665 --> 00:08:13,435
Because every design choice
has a consequence somewhere.

134
00:08:16,075 --> 00:08:20,545
Windows lets the program that opens a
file decide how others may share it.

135
00:08:21,575 --> 00:08:23,935
The cost is that sometimes
you cannot delete your

136
00:08:24,015 --> 00:08:27,385
own file, because another
program has not allowed it.

137
00:08:28,345 --> 00:08:31,185
Unix keeps the name and
the object apart, so many

138
00:08:31,265 --> 00:08:33,795
things can be replaced
while the system is running.

139
00:08:34,985 --> 00:08:36,685
The cost is half a gigabyte that vanished

140
00:08:36,765 --> 00:08:39,655
from the disk and belongs
to no file at all.

141
00:08:42,445 --> 00:08:44,855
And look at how each
of those gets resolved.

142
00:08:45,765 --> 00:08:49,535
On Windows, one way out is closing
the program that holds the file.

143
00:08:50,715 --> 00:08:53,615
On Linux, one way to get
that disk space back is

144
00:08:53,695 --> 00:08:56,265
restarting the service
that still has it open.

145
00:08:57,505 --> 00:09:00,135
Different causes.
Sometimes, the same remedy.

146
00:09:03,265 --> 00:09:04,845
By the way, if you'd like to see where the

147
00:09:04,925 --> 00:09:07,675
next question takes us,
consider subscribing.

148
00:09:08,525 --> 00:09:09,995
It really helps the channel.

149
00:09:11,385 --> 00:09:13,235
So. The question from the start.

150
00:09:14,105 --> 00:09:15,795
What does it mean to open a file?

151
00:09:16,595 --> 00:09:18,385
It means you are holding something.

152
00:09:19,055 --> 00:09:21,885
The two systems simply hand
you different things to hold.

153
00:09:22,985 --> 00:09:27,075
Which is why that message never actually
said this file cannot be deleted.

154
00:09:28,355 --> 00:09:31,465
It said the program that opened
it did not agree to let you.

155
00:09:32,465 --> 00:09:34,155
And it does know which program.

156
00:09:34,765 --> 00:09:36,255
It just did not mention it.
